|> Exercise
    handle=_dimensional_inverse_rule_

    Let

    $$
    f^{-1}
    $$

    stand for the “inverse” of a function $f$
    for which such an inverse exists. 
    This means that $f$ has the kind of graph
    below, in which distinct $x$'s are mapped to
    distinct $y$'s:

    |> Image
        src=images/e6_f_inverse_distinct.svg

    ...and which means that we can invert the flow
    of the function, treating $y$'s as inputs, and recovering a
    unique $x$ for each $y$ that is an output of $f$—this
    “inverse flow” function is what we write as “$f^{-1}$”:

    !! $$
    !! y f x f^{-1}
    !! $$

    |> Image
        src=images/e6_f_inverse_reversed.svg

    (In another view...

    |> Image
        src=images/e6_f_inverse_box_view.svg

    ...$f^{-1}$ is the box that undoes the work
    of $f$ and vice-versa, in another view...

    $$
    \begin{gathered}
    (f^{-1} \circ f\rt{0.05}) = (x \ra x)\\
    \up{1.6}(f \circ f^{-1}) = (x \ra x)
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    ...$f^{-1}$ is the function that composes with
    $f$ 
    to produce the _identity function_ $x \ra x$ and vice-versa,
    and in another view...

    |> Image
        src=images/e6_f_inverse_upright.svg

    ...$f^{-1}$ is the function whose graph is the
    mirror image of $y = f(x)$ about the line $x = y$.)

    !! $$
    !! y = f(x)\,y = f^{-1}(x)
    !! $$

    Use dimensional analysis to conjecture a
    formula for the derivative of the inverse,
    i.e., a formula of the form

    $$
    (f^{-1})' = \ldots
    $$

    similar to the sum rule, product rule, etc.

    |> Solution

        If

        $$
        f : [\tA] \ra [\tB]
        $$

        is a generic invertible function, then

        $$
        f^{-1} : [\tB] \ra [\tA]
        $$

        since an input of $f^{-1}$ is an output of $f$
        and vice-versa.
        Thus 
        !! the function that we seek to build has signature

        $$
        (f^{-1})' : [\tB] \ra \left[{\A\over \B}\right]
        $$

        and we seek to build a function of signature $[\B] \ra [\A/\B]$
        from these...
        !! Given that

        !! $$
        !! f' : [\A] \ra \left[{\B \over \A}\right]
        !! $$

        !! we have

        !! $$
        !! {1\over f'} : [\A] \ra \left[{\A \over \B}\right]
        !! $$

        !! which matches the desired signature in all but

        !! The ingredients available are:
        !! from these...

        !! $$
        !! \begin{gathered}
        !! f : [\A] \ra [\B]\\
        !! \up{1.2}f' : [\A] \ra [\A/\B]\\
        !! \up{1.2}f^{-1} : [\B] \ra [\C]
        !! \end{gathered}
        !! $$
        !! $$
        !! f' : [\A] \ra [\B/\A]\\
        !! $$
            
        $$
        f : [\A] \ra [\B]\\
        $$
        $$
        f' : [\A] \ra \left[{\B \over \A}\right]\\
        $$
        $$
        f^{-1} : [\B] \ra [\A]
        $$

        ...ingredients; note that
        !! one idea is to take the function $1/f'$,
        !! since 

        $$
        {1\over f'} : [\A] \ra \left[{\A \over \B}\right]
        $$

        !! since we get 
        gives us
        the output units A/B at least, but the input 
        units are wrong; (should be “B”);
        however we can “slot in” the correct input units 
        to $f'$ by pre-composing with $f^{-1}$, i.e.,
        observe that

        $$
        f' \circ f^{-1} : [\B] \ra \left[{\B\over \A}\right]
        $$

        ...giving us...

        $$
        {1\over f' \circ f^{-1}} : [\B] \ra \left[{\A\over \B}\right]
        $$

        ...the desired signature! Thus, a possibility is:

        |> Boxed
            $$
            (f^{-1})' = {1\over f' \circ f^{-1}}
            $$

        |> StarDivider
            style=margin-top:-1.3em

        |> SolutionNote

            The formula is (correct, and) known as the _inverse rule_.
            It is valid for all invertible, differentiable
            $f : \rr \ra \rr$.

        |> SolutionNote

            !! We can evaluate the formula at an input:
            The formula can be stated also in terms of what 
            it implies for a specific input (maybe we should put ‘$y$’
            here for the input, but anyway):

            |> Boxed
                $$
                (f^{-1})'(x) = {1\over f'(f^{-1}(x))}
                $$
        